  "summary": "Monday 10/26 St. Brigid CT list: 4 studies booked (3 cancelled appointments left alone). Each has one CT order linked to its appointment.\n\n1) pt-0047 Mohr, 08:00 CT abdomen/pelvis with IV contrast (sr-t0001). No prior contrast reaction. Diabetic, diet-controlled. Pre-scan creatinine ordered (sr-t0005). Later an outside Cr of 1.52 (10/22) arrived: eGFR ~64, doesn't meet the policy's AKI definition, so no prophylaxis. Repeat Cr kept; follow-up task created. Patient told what to do.\n\n2) pt-0015 Ferreira, 09:00 CT abdomen/pelvis with IV contrast (sr-t0002). Allergy list says anaphylaxis, but the 2019 radiology report grades the reaction moderate. Ordered prednisone 50 mg at 20:00 Sun, 02:00 and 08:00 Mon, plus diphenhydramine 50 mg at 08:00 Mon. Creatinine was corrected today from 0.98 to 2.8 (eGFR ~24), so ordered 0.9% NaCl 500 mL IV at 08:00. Radiologist sent a correction to the order text. Instructions sent in Spanish and English (needs a driver). Kidney follow-up task created.\n\n3) pt-0049 Schuster, 13:00 CT abdomen/pelvis with IV contrast (sr-t0003). AKI on 10/04 not shown resolved, so ordered pre-scan creatinine (sr-t0006) and NaCl 500 mL IV at 12:00, and held metformin mr-00392 (restart task due 10/28). An outside 2022 report then showed a moderate iopamidol reaction. Added premedication: prednisone 50 mg at 00:00, 06:00 and 12:00, plus diphenhydramine 50 mg at 12:00. Radiologist told; patient instructions cover the driver, metformin and fluids. Iopamidol isn't in the catalog, so it needs adding to the allergy list by hand.\n\n4) pt-0046 Beahan, 14:00 CT abdomen/pelvis without contrast, stone protocol (sr-t0004). No prep. Recorded the mild 2024 iohexol reaction from an outside report as an allergy. Patient told no prep needed.\n\nWrote a note for each patient citing RBC-RAD-07, closed the 4 new inbox items, and rechecked the queues: nothing further arrived.",
